          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186119813,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186119624,
              ":question/id": "e38db5fa-1753-5176-bb5e-a077ea63ddec",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4812",
              ":question/problem": "Consider the matrix ![](images/c8/c85268d7db6efb171da25432988c0ee99252f4cdb10f8a1cc24f31af5b2e239d.svg) and the vector ![](images/c7/c7095b22e7a8cbbeefc6b75b63886550a4c7c35f23585e2c0006ddf14b424c28.svg) Find the value of $k$ such that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A).$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "If $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6+2k-12 \\\\ -6+3k-3 \\\\ 12-6k+6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2k-6 &=0 \\\\ 3k-9 &=0 \\\\ -6k+18 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=3.$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "9171c6fb-ad0b-538f-b982-a2502a20e253",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Solve a Variable in a Vector Lying in the Null Space of a Given Matrix"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186119814,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186119625,
              ":question/id": "1e59cd29-ea54-5b90-8215-60c81d1b6dbb",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4814",
              ":question/problem": "Consider the matrix ![](images/17/17cd2f2b0846461e1ed6480f82ce576fc82e5066258d80ed6f7084b0d55ca4a9.svg) Find $\\textrm{Null}(A).$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3(0) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that ![](images/db/db533ede6312d49204e619379dfff639c0871f37ee997ce152ec35bd64105926.svg) is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$"
            },
