            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186124317,
              ":question/id": "e1036a11-ebb3-594d-aba1-be259eb0596d",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5419",
              ":question/problem": "If $A$ is a $9 \\times 11$ matrix that has $9$ pivot columns, then what is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))?$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is the number of non-pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $11$ columns and $9$ of them are pivots, then the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n11-9=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=2$."
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "ce5ddb87-f7c0-5bda-8d6e-ac1728dd2f99",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Determining the Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix Given the Number of Non-Pivot Columns"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186124509,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186124318,
              ":question/id": "4aa47a89-9f9b-55d8-ba01-a131b20e6c95",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5421",
              ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If ![](images/77/77c0dd8d9b925b3310c5470f272b59e2b9d25bb141295741f1748771b6c454c1.svg) then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=2$\n\nII. If ![](images/86/86c4f440750b5d7bf45e8c36b6fd9852c2749ac56331b3e0d76a6901acaf5b60.svg) then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(B))=0$\n\nIII. If ![](images/22/2295be7f70c3de36b51784b2919af95695f5520abdb2300287a17852b4cbfce3.svg) then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(C))=1$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(B))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186124510,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186124319,
              ":question/id": "42d77ab8-0670-55bf-8b99-68270c8e5f2e",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5422",
              ":question/problem": "Given ![](images/82/829e66e040815801f77e92b2afd97531779864935faf7c9dcf1b0485593db856.svg) what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A))$, we reduce $A$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A)) = 1.\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "f3a0e3b9-931a-52bd-9ed9-b8001c8de67b",
